Security & Sharing Model (Salesforce)

This layer defines who can see what data and what they can do with it. Salesforce security is built in layers, from broad access → fine-grained control.

7.1 Object Level Security

Controls what objects a user can access and what actions they can perform.

Profiles
Base level of access assigned to every user.
Defines:
Object permissions:
Create
Read
Edit
Delete (CRUD)
Field-level security
Login hours/IP restrictions
One profile per user (mandatory)

Example:

Sales User → Read/Edit on Opportunity
Support User → Read-only on Opportunity
Permission Sets
Used to extend access, not restrict.
A user can have multiple permission sets.

Why use them?

Avoid creating too many profiles
Provide flexible access

Example:

Profile: Read-only on Accounts
Permission Set: Edit access → assigned only to specific users
Key Concept
Profiles = baseline
Permission Sets = additional permissions
7.2 Field Level Security (FLS)

Controls visibility of individual fields within an object.

Two Controls:
Visible / Hidden
Read-Only / Editable
Example:
Salary field:
HR → Visible & Editable
Manager → Visible (Read-only)
Employee → Hidden
Important Notes:
FLS overrides page layouts
Even if field is on UI → if FLS denies → user cannot see it
Applies to:
UI
Reports
API
7.3 Record Level Security

Controls which specific records a user can access.

OWD (Org-Wide Defaults)

Baseline level of record access.

Setting	Meaning
Private	Only owner can access
Public Read Only	Others can view
Public Read/Write	Full access
Controlled by Parent	Inherited
Example:
Account OWD = Private
→ Only owner can see their accounts
Role Hierarchy
Provides vertical access
Higher roles can see records owned by lower roles

Example:

CEO
→ Sales Manager
→ Sales Rep

CEO can see everything below.

Sharing Rules
Provide automatic access between groups
Types:
Owner-based
Criteria-based
Example:
Share all Accounts where Region = "South" with Support Team
Manual Sharing
Record owner can share a specific record manually
Key Idea:
OWD = baseline
Role Hierarchy = vertical access
Sharing Rules = horizontal access
7.4 Apex Security

When using Apex, Salesforce does NOT enforce security automatically → you must handle it.

with sharing / without sharing
with sharing
Enforces:
Record-level security
Respects:
OWD
Sharing rules
public with sharing class MyClass {
}
without sharing
Ignores record-level security
Runs in system context
public without sharing class MyClass {
}
Key Difference:
Mode	Record Access
with sharing	Respects user access
without sharing	Full access (system mode)
stripInaccessible()

Used to enforce:

Field-level security
Object-level security
Example:
List<Account> accs = [SELECT Name FROM Account];

SObjectAccessDecision decision = Security.stripInaccessible(
    AccessType.READABLE,
    accs
);

List<Account> safeList = decision.getRecords();
What it does:
Removes fields user should not access
Prevents security violations in Apex
Final Layered View (Very Important)
1. Object Level → Can user access object?
2. Field Level → Can user see/edit fields?
3. Record Level → Can user access this record?
4. Apex Security → Enforce all above in code
Interview Insight
Most common mistake:
→ “Apex respects security by default” ❌ (Wrong)
Correct:
→ You must explicitly enforce security using:
with sharing
stripInaccessible()